WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.242
Worked examples are powerful because they let learners study a correct problem-solving

00:00:04.242 --> 00:00:07.003
pattern before they are responsible for every step.

00:00:07.003 --> 00:00:10.799
For beginners, unguided problem solving too early can overload working memory.

00:00:10.799 --> 00:00:14.941
They may spend so much effort searching for a path that little

00:00:14.941 --> 00:00:17.012
attention remains for understanding the principle.

00:00:17.012 --> 00:00:21.499
A good example shows both the action and the reasoning behind the action.

00:00:21.499 --> 00:00:23.915
Example-problem pairs are a practical design pattern.

00:00:23.915 --> 00:00:25.641
Learners study a worked example,

00:00:25.641 --> 00:00:29.437
then solve a similar problem with enough variation to require attention.

00:00:29.437 --> 00:00:33.924
This helps them compare the structure of the example with their own attempt.

00:00:33.924 --> 00:00:37.030
Feedback then connects the attempt back to the pattern.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:41.172
Over time, this builds a bridge from guided understanding to independent performance.

00:00:41.172 --> 00:00:44.623
Fading guidance means gradually reducing support as learners gain capability.

00:00:44.623 --> 00:00:47.039
Early practice may include fully worked examples.

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:50.836
Later practice may include partially completed steps, prompts, hints, or checklists.

00:00:50.836 --> 00:00:53.252
Eventually, learners take on the whole task.

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:55.668
The shift should be based on readiness,

00:00:55.668 --> 00:00:59.119
not on an arbitrary desire to make the course harder.

00:00:59.119 --> 00:01:02.571
Removing support too early can increase load without improving learning.

00:01:02.571 --> 00:01:04.987
Practice should change as learners gain experience.

00:01:04.987 --> 00:01:07.058
Beginners need structure, examples, and feedback.

00:01:07.058 --> 00:01:10.854
More advanced learners need challenge, variation, and opportunities to make decisions.

00:01:10.854 --> 00:01:14.996
The design problem is balancing support and challenge so learners are not

00:01:14.996 --> 00:01:19.138
left guessing at the beginning and are not trapped in over-explained exercises

00:01:19.138 --> 00:01:19.788
later.

00:01:19.788 --> 00:01:22.549
Good practice helps learners build schemas, test them,

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:24.660
and use them under increasingly realistic conditions.
